WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.805
Picture copying the addresses from an old address book into a new one.

2
00:00:06.805 --> 00:00:09.092
You keep your place with a sticky note.

3
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:11.587
You've copied four when the doorbell rings.

4
00:00:11.587 --> 00:00:16.457
While you're away, a friend carries on from the sticky note, which still marks the start.

5
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:19.259
The friend copies from the note to the end.

6
00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:21.884
How many addresses are in the new book now?

7
00:00:21.884 --> 00:00:22.861
Pause and guess.

8
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.529
Ten, for six people.

9
00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:28.886
Four of them twice.

10
00:00:28.886 --> 00:00:30.743
Nobody did anything wrong.

11
00:00:30.743 --> 00:00:33.529
The sticky note stayed behind the work.

12
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.313
The café payments program does the same.

13
00:00:36.313 --> 00:00:42.844
It reads card payments from Kafka, a program that keeps logs of messages for other programs to read.

14
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:50.081
For each payment, it writes a ledger entry, a line in the book of money movements, to a log called ledger-entries.

15
00:00:50.081 --> 00:00:51.751
Then it moves its bookmark.

16
00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:54.823
Kafka keeps that bookmark in a log of its own.

17
00:00:54.823 --> 00:01:00.728
So these are two separate writes, often on different machines, and nothing ties them together.

18
00:01:00.728 --> 00:01:05.628
A crash in between, and the lesson's demo ends with 10 entries for 6 payments.

19
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.862
You might be thinking: Kafka has an idempotent producer, which drops repeats.

20
00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:15.200
It drops one kind: the same producer sending the same batch twice.

21
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:19.440
The friend who took over is a new producer with a new id.

22
00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.345
To Kafka, its four entries are brand new.

23
00:01:22.345 --> 00:01:24.188
Same face, total stranger.

24
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:28.444
What we want is for the entries and the bookmark to be one write.

25
00:01:28.444 --> 00:01:30.012
All of it, or none of it.

26
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.783
Back to the address book.

27
00:01:31.783 --> 00:01:36.848
Suppose you copy each address in pencil, and move the sticky note in pencil too.

28
00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:42.845
At the end of the batch you either ink the whole lot with one stamp, or cross the whole lot out.

29
00:01:42.845 --> 00:01:46.113
A visitor who trusts only ink never sees half a batch.

30
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:50.870
That's a transaction: a group of writes that count together or not at all.

31
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.969
Kafka needs three things to do it.

32
00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:58.414
First, a referee who remembers which logs a transaction touched, and how it ended.

33
00:01:58.414 --> 00:02:01.166
Kafka calls it the transaction coordinator.

34
00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:06.349
One of Kafka's servers takes on the job, and keeps its notes in a log of its own.

35
00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:10.748
Second, a name for each copy of the program, such as ledger-0.

36
00:02:10.748 --> 00:02:18.975
It's called the transactional id, and it stays the same across restarts, so Kafka knows the next copy is the same program.

37
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.036
Third, five calls, in order.

38
00:02:21.036 --> 00:02:23.277
Let's replay the crash with them.

39
00:02:23.277 --> 00:02:29.052
initTransactions, once at start-up: the copy registers its name with the coordinator.

40
00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:34.467
beginTransaction: from now on, everything it sends belongs to the transaction.

41
00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:35.379
Then send.

42
00:02:35.379 --> 00:02:36.736
Here's the surprise.

43
00:02:36.736 --> 00:02:41.486
The entries go into the log straight away, before anything is decided.

44
00:02:41.486 --> 00:02:43.793
They're in pencil, but they're in.

45
00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:45.253
And now the crash.

46
00:02:45.253 --> 00:02:48.396
Instance 1 dies with four entries in pencil.

47
00:02:48.867 --> 00:02:51.458
Instance 2 starts with the same name.

48
00:02:51.458 --> 00:02:57.832
Its initTransactions reaches the coordinator, which finishes what the older copy left open.

49
00:02:57.832 --> 00:02:58.743
It aborts it.

50
00:02:58.933 --> 00:03:00.588
An abort deletes nothing.

51
00:03:00.588 --> 00:03:07.075
It adds a marker after the four, a small record that tells careful readers: skip what came before.

52
00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:13.119
Instance 2 begins, reads from the bookmark at 0, and sends six entries, in pencil.

53
00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:17.224
sendOffsetsToTransaction: the bookmark joins the transaction.

54
00:03:17.224 --> 00:03:21.816
It's in pencil too, and the program has turned off saving it on its own.

55
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:23.171
commitTransaction.

56
00:03:23.171 --> 00:03:26.361
The coordinator writes its decision in its notes.

57
00:03:26.361 --> 00:03:33.455
Then it puts a COMMIT marker into every log the transaction touched: the entries, and the bookmark's log too.

58
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:36.076
One stamp, and the whole batch is in ink.

59
00:03:36.076 --> 00:03:38.915
The most powerful rubber stamp in the building.

60
00:03:38.915 --> 00:03:43.625
So the point is: one decision makes six entries and a bookmark count together.

61
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:46.283
So ledger-entries holds ten entries.

62
00:03:46.283 --> 00:03:48.119
How many does a reader see?

63
00:03:48.119 --> 00:03:49.208
Pause and guess.

64
00:03:52.433 --> 00:03:54.048
It depends on the reader.

65
00:03:54.048 --> 00:04:00.247
A careful reader, set to read_committed, skips the aborted four and sees six, each payment once.

66
00:04:00.247 --> 00:04:04.187
A reader left on the default, read_uncommitted, sees all ten.

67
00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:11.264
So every program that reads ledger-entries must ask for read_committed, or the transaction protects nothing.

68
00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:12.423
Two more habits.

69
00:04:12.423 --> 00:04:15.951
After an abort, seek back to the first record of the batch.

70
00:04:15.951 --> 00:04:19.719
Reading the batch already moved the program's position past it.

71
00:04:19.719 --> 00:04:24.443
Without the seek, the next read skips the batch, and it's never written at all.

72
00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:27.439
And keep transactions short, one batch each.

73
00:04:27.439 --> 00:04:33.433
Every transaction costs a few extra round trips to the coordinator, so one per record is slow.

74
00:04:33.433 --> 00:04:36.493
And while one is open, the careful readers wait.

75
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.608
Here's one batch, in Java and in Kotlin.

76
00:04:39.833 --> 00:04:42.933
Begin, send the entries, send the bookmark, commit.

77
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.960
If anything fails, abort, and seek back.

78
00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:53.276
But catch ProducerFencedException first: it means a newer copy has taken over the name, so this one must stop.

79
00:04:53.867 --> 00:04:56.881
So, how do you stop four entries landing twice?

80
00:04:56.881 --> 00:04:59.702
Make the entries and the bookmark one write.

81
00:04:59.702 --> 00:05:05.345
Everything goes in pencil, then one stamp inks it all, or one marker crosses it all out.

82
00:05:05.345 --> 00:05:07.461
And every reader trusts only ink.

83
00:05:07.633 --> 00:05:08.782
One quick check.

84
00:05:08.782 --> 00:05:12.944
A program writes 14 entries in a transaction, then aborts.

85
00:05:12.944 --> 00:05:17.394
Another program reads the topic with Kafka's default settings.

86
00:05:17.394 --> 00:05:18.614
What does it see?

87
00:05:18.614 --> 00:05:19.763
Pause and think.

88
00:05:22.967 --> 00:05:23.465
All 14.

89
00:05:23.465 --> 00:05:29.016
The abort only added a marker, and the default reader doesn't look at markers.

90
00:05:29.016 --> 00:05:30.368
Set read_committed.
